      en: "The Port of Morondava, situated on the Mozambique Channel, is the primary maritime hub for the Menabe region. It is predominantly a regional port, heavily focused on supporting the lucrative local fishing and aquaculture industries. Morondava is the base for a massive commercial shrimping fleet, which exports high-value frozen prawns to European and Asian markets. The port also handles coastal cargo vessels, known as boutres, which transport agricultural goods and timber from isolated communities along the western coast. The infrastructure is relatively basic and faces severe threats from relentless coastal erosion, which has required continuous, costly reinforcements to prevent the sea from reclaiming the harbor facilities. Geografi K6 — fishing ports and coastal erosion."
